1
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:20,540
On 9 October 1967, in a mud-walled schoolhouse
in the Bolivian village of La Higuera,

2
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,740
a prisoner was shot dead by a
sergeant of the Bolivian army.

3
00:00:25,595 --> 00:00:29,900
He was thirty-nine years old, an Argentine
doctor turned revolutionary commander,

4
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:31,880
and the world knew him as Che.

5
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:36,167
Today a photograph of his
face, taken in Havana in 1960,

6
00:00:36,487 --> 00:00:39,827
is printed on T-shirts, flags
and posters on every continent.

7
00:00:40,407 --> 00:00:43,772
To admirers he is a doctor who gave
up comfort to fight for the poor.

8
00:00:44,432 --> 00:00:49,029
To critics, mostly Cuban exiles,
he is the butcher of La Cabaña.

9
00:00:49,474 --> 00:00:51,259
This film does not pick a side.

10
00:00:51,839 --> 00:00:54,799
It sets his own recorded
words beside the documents,

11
00:00:55,099 --> 00:00:56,579
and ends with one question.

12
00:00:57,325 --> 00:01:01,110
Ernesto Guevara was born
in Rosario, Argentina,

13
00:01:01,610 --> 00:01:07,510
in 1928, and qualified as a doctor in 1953,
after a nine-month motorcycle journey around

14
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:08,190
South America.

15
00:01:08,861 --> 00:01:16,561
In Mexico he joined Fidel Castro's rebels,
and on 2 December 1956 he landed in Cuba with

16
00:01:16,561 --> 00:01:20,301
eighty other men, in an expedition
that was almost wiped out.

17
00:01:20,789 --> 00:01:25,334
Two years later, in January
1959, the dictator Batista fled,

18
00:01:25,674 --> 00:01:26,854
and the rebels entered Havana.

19
00:01:36,228 --> 00:01:38,373
Guevara was thirty years old.

20
00:01:38,953 --> 00:01:41,633
Within days, Castro put him
in command of a fortress.

21
00:01:47,667 --> 00:01:50,947
The fortress was La Cabaña,
above Havana harbour.

22
00:01:51,827 --> 00:01:57,607
Guevara commanded it for five months,
from 2 January to 12 June 1959.

23
00:01:58,292 --> 00:02:02,632
Officers and police of the old regime were
tried there by revolutionary tribunals,

24
00:02:03,052 --> 00:02:04,792
and Guevara reviewed the appeals.

25
00:02:05,732 --> 00:02:08,312
Some defendants were sentenced
to death by firing squad.

26
00:02:08,941 --> 00:02:14,426
In January 1959, an American newsreel reported
that one million people had gathered at

27
00:02:14,426 --> 00:02:17,426
the presidential palace to shout
their approval of the executions.

28
00:02:30,538 --> 00:02:33,133
How many men died under his
command is still argued.

29
00:02:33,833 --> 00:02:37,393
His biographer Jon Lee Anderson
counts 55 at La Cabaña.

30
00:02:37,933 --> 00:02:40,793
The exile group Cuba Archive counts 105.

31
00:02:41,518 --> 00:02:46,883
A Cuban justice ministry adviser wrote in
1991 that the tribunals followed the Nuremberg

32
00:02:46,883 --> 00:02:53,503
model, and the biographer Paco Ignacio Taibo
reports a poll at the time that found 93

33
00:02:53,503 --> 00:02:54,243
percent approval.

34
00:02:54,897 --> 00:02:59,257
Critics answer that the crowds chanted for the
firing wall without respect for due process,

35
00:02:59,757 --> 00:03:05,877
as Castañeda puts it, and Cuba Archive says
the 105 deaths were carried out without due

36
00:03:05,877 --> 00:03:06,777
process of law.

37
00:03:07,305 --> 00:03:13,230
Anderson wrote in 1997 that he had never found
a credible case in which Guevara executed

38
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:14,250
an innocent man.

39
00:03:14,781 --> 00:03:17,041
Guevara did not hide from the subject.

40
00:03:17,721 --> 00:03:22,781
On 11 December 1964, answering delegates
at the United Nations in New York,

41
00:03:23,081 --> 00:03:26,481
he said that Cuba had executed
people, was executing them,

42
00:03:26,761 --> 00:03:28,481
and would go on as long as necessary.

43
00:03:29,171 --> 00:03:32,491
In the same reply he said Cuba
did not commit assassinations,

44
00:03:32,971 --> 00:03:35,631
a charge he turned on the
political police of Venezuela.

45
00:03:36,407 --> 00:03:39,987
He had also delivered Cuba's main
address to the General Assembly that day.

46
00:03:52,833 --> 00:03:54,033
Both things are in the record:

47
00:03:54,553 --> 00:03:58,233
a tribunal system that Cuba defended
and that many Cubans backed,

48
00:03:58,653 --> 00:04:00,913
and a commander who defended
the shooting in public.

49
00:04:01,613 --> 00:04:04,313
The record does not settle
whether the trials were fair.

50
00:04:10,195 --> 00:04:14,580
Afterwards he helped run Cuba's economy, at
the national bank and then as minister of

51
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:14,940
industries.

52
00:04:15,657 --> 00:04:19,597
In August 1961, at a conference
of American states in Uruguay,

53
00:04:20,017 --> 00:04:20,817
he spoke for Cuba.

54
00:04:35,263 --> 00:04:40,913
A CIA analysis written in October 1965 said
that the economy had fallen to its lowest

55
00:04:40,913 --> 00:04:42,813
point since Castro came to power.

56
00:04:43,413 --> 00:04:45,493
That is an American intelligence view.

57
00:04:46,067 --> 00:04:52,567
By then Guevara had left. In October 1965
Castro read out a letter in which he gave up

58
00:04:52,567 --> 00:04:54,587
his posts and his Cuban citizenship.

59
00:04:55,336 --> 00:05:00,166
He had arrived secretly in the Congo
on 24 April 1965 to help rebels there.

60
00:05:00,946 --> 00:05:04,826
Seven months later he left, and his own
journal calls it the story of a failure.

61
00:05:05,509 --> 00:05:09,429
In November 1966 he entered
Bolivia under a false name,

62
00:05:09,729 --> 00:05:13,169
to begin a guerrilla war that he hoped
would spread across South America.

63
00:05:13,892 --> 00:05:16,652
The Bolivian Communist
Party, led by Mario Monje,

64
00:05:16,912 --> 00:05:19,472
did not back him, and few
local peasants joined.

65
00:05:20,252 --> 00:05:21,312
Some informed on the group.

66
00:05:21,871 --> 00:05:25,176
Meanwhile the Bolivian army was
raising a new ranger battalion.

67
00:05:25,856 --> 00:05:31,896
Under a memorandum of 28 April 1967, a team
of sixteen American Green Berets trained

68
00:05:31,896 --> 00:05:32,116
it.

69
00:05:32,542 --> 00:05:37,482
A Cuban film, which its archive description
says was made within 48 hours of his death,

70
00:05:37,622 --> 00:05:41,702
shows Bolivian streets, soldiers, and
the anti -guerrilla posters of the time.

71
00:05:42,281 --> 00:05:45,666
By August 1967 the
guerrillas were being hunted.

72
00:05:46,326 --> 00:05:50,266
On 7 October an informant told
the army where their camp was,

73
00:05:50,526 --> 00:05:51,726
in the Yuro ravine.

74
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:02,000
On 8 October, about 180
soldiers surrounded the ravine.

75
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:06,620
Guevara was wounded and captured by
rangers led by Captain Gary Prado.

76
00:06:07,613 --> 00:06:11,613
He was taken to La Higuera and held
overnight in the village schoolhouse.

77
00:06:12,233 --> 00:06:14,313
In the morning he spoke
with the schoolteacher,

78
00:06:14,573 --> 00:06:15,633
Julia Cortez.

79
00:06:16,267 --> 00:06:20,307
Then came the order. Prado
says the Bolivian president,

80
00:06:20,667 --> 00:06:25,787
René Barrientos, ordered him killed at once,
to prevent an escape and to avoid a trial.

81
00:06:26,337 --> 00:06:30,677
A Cuban exile working for
the CIA, Félix Rodríguez,

82
00:06:30,897 --> 00:06:38,177
was at La Higuera. A CIA debriefing of 1975
says he passed the order on from the Bolivian

83
00:06:38,177 --> 00:06:38,757
high command.

84
00:06:39,427 --> 00:06:43,967
In that debriefing Rodríguez says he had
been told to do everything possible to keep

85
00:06:43,967 --> 00:06:48,607
Guevara alive, and that the Americans
wanted him flown to Panama for questioning.

86
00:07:02,595 --> 00:07:04,240
The Bolivian officers insisted.

87
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:10,740
A sergeant, Mario Terán, who had volunteered,
fired the shots at about one in the afternoon.

88
00:07:17,428 --> 00:07:20,273
Bolivia's government had planned
to say he died in combat.

89
00:07:20,893 --> 00:07:26,433
On 11 October, President Johnson's adviser
Walt Rostow wrote that the decision to kill

90
00:07:26,433 --> 00:07:27,413
him was stupid.

91
00:07:28,019 --> 00:07:32,284
In 2013 two American authors,
Kevin Maurer and Mitch Weiss,

92
00:07:32,604 --> 00:07:35,064
published a book on the
Green Beret role in the hunt.

93
00:07:35,659 --> 00:07:39,019
What nobody disputes is that
American-trained rangers made the capture,

94
00:07:39,539 --> 00:07:41,279
and that a CIA man was present.

95
00:07:42,059 --> 00:07:44,599
Whether Washington could have
stopped it, or wanted to,

96
00:07:44,979 --> 00:07:45,759
is still argued.

97
00:07:54,328 --> 00:07:59,753
Also on 18 October Fidel Castro told a crowd
in Havana that those who sing victory over

98
00:07:59,753 --> 00:08:00,853
his death are mistaken.

99
00:08:01,629 --> 00:08:04,344
His burial place was kept
secret for thirty years.

100
00:08:04,984 --> 00:08:11,524
In July 1997 a Cuban and Argentine team found
remains near the airstrip at Vallegrande,

101
00:08:11,524 --> 00:08:17,624
and on 17 October 1997 they were laid
to rest in a mausoleum in Santa Clara,

102
00:08:17,784 --> 00:08:17,944
Cuba.

103
00:08:18,650 --> 00:08:22,430
A fortress in 1959, a schoolhouse in 1967.

104
00:08:23,310 --> 00:08:24,690
Which of the two men is on the poster?
